  "notes": "The other half of the duo, and deliberately the inverse of it: \"the softer, more harmonically delicate and more acoustic-facing voice\", weighted very slow with a large drumless fraction. So `bpm` is the entry's straight 62–92 with the mode low, the kit is \"a soft kick on 1, a quiet snap or rim on 2&4, almost no hats, and long fully drumless sections\", and where a trap kit enters \"it is low-passed and mixed behind the vocals\" — hence `lowPassedClap`, a hat carpet near zero and the quietest `velocityTiers` in the section. The harmonic claim is unusual and is authored as written: \"the harmonic interest comes from **vocal stacking creating the chord** rather than from an instrumental bed — model the harmony as generated by the voices\", which is why `countermelody.densityRatio` is the highest in this batch and the chords block holds long extended sonorities rather than motion. \"Melody is light, breathy, high soprano at low volume, low note-density, long phrases\" is the melody block; the entry's \"close block harmony (3–6 parts a third apart)\" cannot be spelled in `countermelody`, which offers only an octave-class offset, so the stack is modelled as a dense octave echo and that limitation is recorded here rather than faked. ⚠ `confidence` is **low**, mirroring medium-low: \"tempo and drum values are inference\". ⚠ All drum, 808 and density numbers are **INFERRED**. ⚠ `session.bpm.mode` is **INFERRED**: the number is not stated in the entry, and it is an authored centre of gravity inside the band the entry does state. ⚠ **`session.keys` is INFERRED.** The entry's Keys line describes the harmonic grammar but names no key centre, so these values and their weights are an authored choice — no key in this set is stated in the research.",
